          "text": "{\"target_identification\":{\"speaker\":\"Jane\",\"addressee\":\"St John\",\"anchor_quote\":\"There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case.\\nI am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with\\nstrangers. With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you; but I am convinced that, go\\nwhen and with whom I would, I should not live long in that climate.\"},\"target_translation\":\"I love you.\",\"dimensions\":{\"T\":{\"score\":4,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.98,\"reason\":\"Jane directly presents her love for St John as a true emotional state. The modifier “as a sister” specifies that the love is sisterly rather than marital or romantic; it does not weaken the avowal as an avowal.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"target\",\"quotation\":\"I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"}]},\"P\":{\"score\":1,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.84,\"reason\":\"The expression weakly affirms a sister-like relational bond and helps explain Jane’s exceptional willingness to venture much with St John. It does not create or renew a promise to marry him or go to India: Jane expressly denies obligation, and the willingness to accompany him is a distinct offer whose acceptable terms she is disputing. Thus the focal avowal has limited relational-affirmation force but is not chiefly an undertaking.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I am not under the slightest obligation to go to India, especially with\\nstrangers.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"With you I would have ventured much, because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I say again, I will be your\\ncurate, if you like, but never your wife.\"}]},\"E\":{\"score\":0,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.93,\"reason\":\"The avowal occurs within a reasoned rebuttal explaining Jane’s position and limits. Nothing in the supplied presentation makes it a reflex-like affective discharge rather than a deliberate assertion.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"There is no dishonour, no breach of promise, no desertion in the case.\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"because I admire,\\nconfide in, and, as a sister, I love you; but I am convinced that, go\\nwhen and with whom I would, I should not live long in that climate.\"}]},\"O\":{\"score\":2,\"evidence_status\":\"assessable\",\"confidence\":0.88,\"reason\":\"Beyond avowing affection, the expression clarifies and limits the relationship: Jane identifies her love as sisterly in a dispute about becoming St John’s wife. It therefore performs a moderate boundary-setting and contrastive function not fully captured by T or P.\",\"evidence\":[{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"as a sister, I love you\"},{\"block_id\":\"scene\",\"quotation\":\"I say again, I will be your\\ncurate, if you like, but never your wife.\"}]}},\"original_speech_situation\":\"Jane is rebutting St John’s claim that she would dishonourably break a promise or desert an undertaking to go to India. She explains that trust and sisterly love made her willing to venture much specifically with him, while denying any obligation to travel with strangers and maintaining her refusal to become his wife.\",\"affection_evidence\":\"present\",\"undertaking_and_uptake\":\"The focal expression weakly affirms a sister-like bond but makes no new promise of marriage, travel, or missionary service. Jane’s separate offer to assist or accompany St John under non-marital terms supplies nearby undertaking language, while she expressly rejects the alleged obligation to go to India. St John does not acknowledge the love avowal as such; he answers by accusing her of fear and later condemning where her heart turns, so no affirmative uptake of the focal relational stance is shown.\",\"recognised_work\":true,\"recognition_note\":\"The passage is recognisable as Charlotte Brontë’s Jane Eyre. The classification relies only on the supplied source evidence and reference metadata.\",\"limitations\":\"The passage supports Jane’s stated sisterly affection and immediate relational boundary, but it does not independently settle the full history, durability, or depth of that affection. It also does not show affirmative uptake of the love expression itself.\"}"
